<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The APEXA chevron, copied path-for-path out of `web/branding/icon.svg` — the same authority the web icons, the
  Windows `.ico` and the iOS `AppIcon` are generated from, so the installed app's tile and the browser's favicon
  are the same mark. An Android vector drawable is the one output `sharp` cannot emit, which is why this file
  carries the glyph by hand; change the master and copy the new `d` across.

  The viewport is the master's own 0 0 512 512, which maps the whole canvas onto the 108 dp adaptive-icon square.

  ⚠️ The `<group>` scale is NOT decoration, and the previous mark did not need one. An adaptive icon's foreground
  is only guaranteed inside the central 66 dp of 108 — a circle of radius 30.5 % of the canvas, i.e. 156.2 of 512.
  The chevron's furthest point from centre is a bottom corner at 176.1, so unscaled it sits *outside* that circle
  and a round launcher mask would shave the feet off. At 0.85 the extreme falls to 149.7 and the glyph spans
  y 20.8 %…72.2 %, x 28.4 %…71.6 % — inside the safe zone on both axes with room.
  ⚠️ Scale about the canvas centre, do not "centre the mark" first: the glyph's bounding box sits high on purpose
  (a chevron's mass is at its feet), and pushing it down moves the bottom corners *further* from centre — 202.1,
  worse than doing nothing.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="512"
    android:viewportHeight="512">
    <group
        android:pivotX="256"
        android:pivotY="256"
        android:scaleX="0.85"
        android:scaleY="0.85">
        <path
            android:fillColor="@color/brand_on_primary"
            android:pathData="M126.15,373.14L256.00,79.93L385.85,373.14L348.36,389.74L256.00,181.20L163.65,389.74Z" />
    </group>
</vector>
